@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — what a transmission is willing to waive for its adepts
#
# One generalization, formed from two attested claims in two transmissions
# with no route between them, and bounded by a third the counter-search found
# in the holding before the node was written.
#
# It is filed on its own because its subject is not the condition but the
# waiver: which of its own requirements a transmission is prepared to call
# dispensable, and on what ground. The holding already carries the shape of
# the calendar-bound condition at pmc:AdmissibilityBoundToTheCalendar; this
# asks what happens at the other end, when a transmission lets somebody out.
#

pmc:AttainmentRelievesOnlyTheOutwardCondition a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "What attainment is allowed to waive is the condition fixed outside the practitioner"@en, "Was die Erlangung erlassen darf, ist die außerhalb des Übenden festgesetzte Bedingung"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where an operative transmission relieves a practitioner of one of its own prescribed conditions on the ground of what he has attained, the condition relieved is one fixed outside him — a day, an hour, a calendar, a star — and not one bearing on his body or his conduct. Where the same transmissions speak of a bodily condition and attainment together, they say that the attainment does not relieve it."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:ClaviculaAdeptiExempti, pmc:ShouyiMianLiri, pmc:ShangqingYijieQingyao ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "The witnesses. The Key of Solomon says that so exact a preparation of days and hours is not necessary for those who are adepts in the art, and gives as the ground that beginners lack faith in the experiments; it waives nothing else, and a search of both books for a corresponding relief from the nine days, the fast, the bath or the confession came back empty. The eighteenth chapter of the Baopuzi says that one who can guard the One need not divine the day or choose the hour, no longer consults the siting calendars, and does not avoid the year star or the taboos of his year and fate. Both waivers are granted for attainment and both fall on the calendar."@en ;
    skos:note "The third witness is the negative half and it is the one that makes the statement more than a coincidence of two. The Zhen'gao states the loss from emission in a countable form and adds that even a person entered in the jade registers is not thereby exempt. There the question of a waiver for the advanced is raised explicitly, about a bodily condition, and answered no."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-search was carried against the compiled holding before this was written, for waivers granted on attainment and for any case in which the thing waived is a rule of the body. The nearest case found does not refute the statement and does bound it: the Mahānirvāṇa Tantra divides practitioners into the bound, the heroic and the divine and makes the division govern which rites a person is competent for, so that the bound one may neither look at a Śūdra nor think of a woman while the heroic one performs a rite with wine, meat, fish, grain and union. That is not one practitioner released from a condition the transmission sets for all; it is a transmission that sets different conditions for different classes from the outset, and the wording above says relieves of one of its own prescribed conditions in order to keep the two apart. Whether the distinction holds under pressure is the first thing a later run should attack."@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a passage in any operative transmission granting an experienced practitioner relief from a bodily or conduct condition — a fast, a term of continence, a washing, a dietary rule — on the ground of his attainment, stated as a waiver of a rule the same text imposes on others. One such passage and the statement is gone. It is deliberately easy to break, because the interest of the statement is entirely in the asymmetry: a transmission that calls its astrology a pedagogy for beginners and will not say the same of its fasting is telling a reader which of the two it believes is doing the work."@en ;
    skos:note "What it does not say. It does not say the waived condition is idle, nor that the unwaived one is effective; both are claims about efficacy and this is a claim about how transmissions talk. And the count is two positive witnesses and one negative, which is thin. The two are separated by nine centuries and by any route anyone has proposed, which is worth something; it is not worth a general law, and the statement is worded as a regularity in what gets waived and not as a rule about all transmissions."@en .
